{
	title: 'Commit cards: a commit a Bash call makes is a card beside its result',
	spec: 'When a Bash call (foreground or background) creates commits (detection: task hy, before the result is written), the history record that carries its result also carries one commit block per commit, after the tool_result: hash, short hash, subject, amend flag, repository directory, files with lines added and removed (one git show --numstat per commit, only when commits exist) and lines. Its id is the record number with a sub-index (e.g. #s357.1, task 9p letter s); no other record carries it, so replay, older pages and links show it as recorded. The transcript draws it as its own card in terminal and web, kind commit with config key blockVerbosityCommit (default short), following short/glimpse/long (task hyt): short is one row, committed <short hash> <subject> (amended for an amend) in the commit color; glimpse and long add the files with +/- counts, then the lines. Clients watching the session therefore see each commit in the transcript; other clients keep the commit notice of task hy. Hook: commits.committed(info) returns the lines (default none); plugins hook it with after/around (task an) to add lines, e.g. a line count or a warning that the commit holds files this session did not change. info names the session, call id, repository, hash, subject, amend and files. Lines from the hook reach the model appended to the Bash result text; without lines the model sees nothing extra. Hook errors go to diag.log and never fail the Bash result. Commits Hal did not observe in a Bash call get no card.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['hy', 'hyt', '9p'],
	foldInto: ['hy']
}
